      "In a variational circuit, the real terrain is the space of quantum states. A small change in one angle might rotate the state a lot, and a small change in another might hardly move it. The Fubini-Study metric tensor records how much the state changes per unit change of each pair of angles. Multiplying the gradient by its inverse, the natural gradient step, moves the parameters so that the state changes by a controlled amount in the most useful direction.",
      "For a single pure state the Fubini-Study metric is one quarter of the quantum Fisher information. Its entries depend on overlaps between derivatives of the state with respect to the parameters. For common layered circuits these can be measured with small modifications of the circuit, using the same type of shifts used for gradients.",
