    lastUpdated: "September 30, 2026",
    overview: [
      "Imaginary-time evolution is a mathematical trick from statistical physics. If you evolve a quantum state under the operator e to the minus tau times H, where H is the Hamiltonian and tau is a kind of imaginary time, every component of the state that is not in the ground state decays, and the weaker the energy, the slower the decay. Run long enough, and only the ground state is left. The catch is that e to the minus tau H is not unitary, so a quantum computer cannot apply it directly.",
      "Quantum imaginary-time evolution, or QITE, approximates this non-unitary evolution with a sequence of short unitary steps chosen by solving small classical linear equations. It was proposed as an alternative to VQE for finding ground states and thermal states on near-term hardware, with the advantage that it does not need a classical optimizer loop over circuit parameters.",
    ],
    history: [
      "Imaginary-time evolution has long been used in classical simulations, such as the power method for finding ground states and quantum Monte Carlo. Motta, Sun, Tan, and coauthors from Caltech and Google posted the QITE algorithm in 2019, and it appeared in Nature Physics in 2020. In parallel, McArdle and coauthors proposed a variational ansatz-based version in npj Quantum Information in 2019, and Yuan and coauthors published a general theory of variational quantum simulation the same year.",
      "The original paper demonstrated QITE on a superconducting qubit device for small Hamiltonians, and later work extended it to thermal states and dynamical properties. Sun and coauthors in 2021 used QITE to compute finite-temperature properties of spin systems, and Motta and coauthors proposed the quantum Lanczos method for faster convergence. Experiments on cloud devices have been used to find ground states of small molecules and spin models.",
    ],
