      "If the variable takes values between 0 and 1, the mean is just the probability of a good outcome after a rotation, and amplitude estimation gives it to error epsilon with about 1 over epsilon runs. The harder case is when values are unbounded. Then a few rare, large values can dominate the mean, and a naive scaling would make the problem look harder than it is.",
      "Montanaro's idea is to split the values into scales, such as values near 1, values near 2, 4, 8, and so on. Each scale is bounded, so amplitude estimation applies. The variance bound limits how much weight the large scales can carry, so only a few scales need accurate estimates. Combining them gives the mean with a cost that depends on the standard deviation rather than the maximum value.",
