      "Later work studied codes with more bits per qubit and with shared randomness, including Hayashi, Iwama, Nishimura, Raymond, and Yamashita in 2006 and 2007, who studied random access coding more fully, and Tavakoli, Hameedi, Marques, and Bourennane in 2015, who extended them to d-level systems. Pawłowski and Żukowski showed in 2010 that shared entanglement improves the codes, and the codes became a tool for semi-device-independent tests of quantum behavior. In 2021 and later, Fuller, Hadfield, and coauthors used QRACs to encode three binary variables per qubit in a quantum relaxation approach to optimization, called quantum random access optimization.",
